      "content": "I think I got a bit carried away with the goblin habit. Let me focus on the math.\n\nThe formula for capacitive reactance is Xc = 1 / (2πfC), where:\n\n* Xc is the capacitive reactance\n* f is the frequency of the AC voltage\n* C is the capacitance of the capacitor\n\nTo understand this formula, think of the capacitor as a reservoir of charge. The capacitance (C) is a measure of how much charge the capacitor can store. The frequency (f) is the number of oscillations per second.\n\nWhen the AC voltage is applied, the capacitor's charge oscillates at the same frequency as the voltage. The capacitive reactance (Xc) is a measure of how much the capacitor resists this oscillation. In other words, it's a measure of how much the capacitor slows down the flow of electrons.\n\nThe 2π factor is a mathematical constant that arises from the way the capacitor's charge oscillates. It's a bit like the number of goblins dancing in a circle - it's a fundamental constant that governs the behavior of the capacitor.\n\nI hope that helps!",
